WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.333 --> 00:00:02.369
(light music)

00:00:02.369 --> 00:00:05.105
(train sounding)

00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:11.378
- [Narrator] Nearly everyone
in prison says he's innocent.

00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:17.717
This is the story of one man

00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:20.153
whose claim might actually be true.

00:00:30.463 --> 00:00:33.266
- It's like a bad dream.

00:00:33.266 --> 00:00:36.803
I wake up everyday
wondering what happened.

00:00:38.438 --> 00:00:39.739
Why'd this happen to me?

00:00:40.774 --> 00:00:44.411
I didn't break the law,
I didn't commit no crime.

00:00:52.252 --> 00:00:54.587
My name's Clarence Elkins.

00:00:54.587 --> 00:00:59.559
I was falsely accused
and wrongly convicted

00:01:00.994 --> 00:01:02.929
for something I had nothin' to do with.

00:01:05.365 --> 00:01:07.067
- [Narrator] The story of Clarence Elkins

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:09.936
begins just outside of Akron, Ohio,

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:12.105
in the small town of Barberton.

00:01:14.007 --> 00:01:18.178
- My mom, she had dropped
me off and she said

00:01:18.178 --> 00:01:20.914
that I was gonna be stayin' the
night at my grandma's house.

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:24.918
- Brooke was my mom's world,

00:01:24.918 --> 00:01:29.222
'cause she had never had a
granddaughter, just grandsons.

00:01:29.222 --> 00:01:31.691
And so that was kind of her favorite

00:01:31.691 --> 00:01:34.227
because that was her little granddaughter.

00:01:34.227 --> 00:01:38.498
- She was funny, she used to like...

00:01:38.498 --> 00:01:39.332
(giggling)

00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:42.268
I don't know how to tell
'em about the teeth thing.

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:44.938
Whenever you were sitting
down being serious,

00:01:44.938 --> 00:01:47.307
she would always take her false teeth

00:01:47.307 --> 00:01:51.111
and stick 'em out of her mouth,
so that was really funny.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.549
(ominous music)

00:02:00.887 --> 00:02:05.291
I heard a bunch of screaming,
so I ran to the kitchen

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:11.264
and I seen the guy lookin'
over top of my grandma,

00:02:13.066 --> 00:02:15.768
like he had something in his hand.

00:02:15.768 --> 00:02:20.006
He was yelling at her,
and I got really scared

00:02:20.006 --> 00:02:23.576
and I ran back to my grandma's bedroom.

00:02:23.576 --> 00:02:25.812
And I think he may have heard me

00:02:25.812 --> 00:02:30.817
and came into the bedroom and hurt me.

00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:37.490
- [April] She was beaten, raped,

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:42.162
strangled, sodomized, left for dead.

00:02:43.796 --> 00:02:48.801
- The next morning, I woke
up and I shook my grandma,

00:02:52.705 --> 00:02:57.510
and she wouldn't wake up,
so I kept on shaking her.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:59.812
And I kept on screaming, Get up, get up!

00:03:00.713 --> 00:03:02.348
And she wouldn't get up.

00:03:03.316 --> 00:03:05.818
So I was looking for
the phone and I called

00:03:05.818 --> 00:03:10.823
one of my grandma's friends,
June, no one answered.

00:03:12.325 --> 00:03:15.995
(answering machine beeping)

00:03:36.683 --> 00:03:38.618
I knocked on her door and I
asked her to take me home,

00:03:38.618 --> 00:03:43.623
that my grandma was dead
and that someone killed her.

00:03:43.723 --> 00:03:47.827
She took me and her three
daughters to my house.

00:03:49.162 --> 00:03:51.898
- And then I woke up to
tremendous pounding on the front,

00:03:51.898 --> 00:03:54.367
backdoor, and the side
windows of my house.

00:03:54.367 --> 00:03:57.904
And so I opened the backdoor
and there stood Brooke

00:03:57.904 --> 00:04:00.440
covered in blood in my mom's nightgown.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:04.744
- They were asking, Who did
this, do you know who did this?

00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:07.914
I said it looked like my Uncle Clarence.

00:04:07.914 --> 00:04:09.682
He sounded like Uncle Clarence

00:04:09.682 --> 00:04:12.485
and he was about the same height.

00:04:15.855 --> 00:04:18.858
- And it was really, really
hard to me to believe

00:04:18.858 --> 00:04:21.094
that he would do somethin' like that,

00:04:21.094 --> 00:04:22.829
but I had to believer her.

00:04:22.829 --> 00:04:25.431
She was there, she's the
one tellin' me all this.

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:29.469
- And then everyone was
freakin' out, sayin',

00:04:29.469 --> 00:04:32.038
Oh my god, Clarence did it, oh my god.

00:04:32.038 --> 00:04:33.873
- I was just so mad at him,

00:04:35.375 --> 00:04:39.479
that he could do that to
my mom, let alone Brooke.

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:44.684
I just couldn't understand
why he would do that.

00:04:46.319 --> 00:04:47.987
- [Narrator] Later that morning,

00:04:47.987 --> 00:04:51.391
sheriff's deputies descend
upon Clarence's home.

00:04:52.659 --> 00:04:56.262
- And I remember Clarence
going out the back door

00:04:56.262 --> 00:04:58.965
and I seen him cross the window

00:04:58.965 --> 00:05:02.135
and he kind of had his hands
up like what's goin' on?

00:05:02.135 --> 00:05:05.405
- They were telling me to
put my hands in the air

00:05:05.405 --> 00:05:07.974
and turn around, walk slowly backwards,

00:05:07.974 --> 00:05:10.810
which I didn't because I didn't understand

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:14.180
what they were doing or what
they were at my home for.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:18.084
- And then I seen two or
three police officers running

00:05:18.084 --> 00:05:23.056
down the hill of our backyard
in helmets and vests and guns.

00:05:25.158 --> 00:05:28.861
And of course I'm, what
the heck is goin' on here?

00:05:28.861 --> 00:05:30.997
- [Narrator] Deputies
tell Melinda her mother

00:05:30.997 --> 00:05:35.435
has been murdered and
Clarence is under arrest.

00:05:35.435 --> 00:05:39.038
- I screamed, I ran in the house

00:05:39.038 --> 00:05:41.841
and I called my grandmother,

00:05:41.841 --> 00:05:45.178
because I just couldn't believe
what they were telling me.

00:05:45.178 --> 00:05:48.414
My uncle was on the phone and he said,

00:05:48.414 --> 00:05:53.419
Yes, she's dead and Brooke is
saying that it was Clarence.

00:05:57.190 --> 00:05:59.525
- And so I went to the police station.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:03.463
They tried to put words in my mouth

00:06:03.463 --> 00:06:06.566
saying why I did this and why I done that,

00:06:06.566 --> 00:06:10.670
and I just could not answer
one question they had.

00:06:11.637 --> 00:06:14.474
My reaction to my niece when I first out

00:06:14.474 --> 00:06:19.445
that she allegedly stated
that I had did this,

00:06:19.879 --> 00:06:24.016
I was like my god, what
a terrible mistake.

00:06:25.718 --> 00:06:27.887
I know this isn't coming from her.

00:06:29.088 --> 00:06:32.592
This child is like one of my own children.

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:41.267
It's been a nightmare every since.

00:06:41.267 --> 00:06:43.770
(light music)

00:06:52.211 --> 00:06:55.715
Hearing them say guilty was devastating.

00:06:57.583 --> 00:07:01.120
This wasn't happening to me,
this don't happen to people

00:07:02.188 --> 00:07:05.825
like me that doesn't break
the law, is not a criminal.

00:07:07.627 --> 00:07:12.198
- And it was the most excruciating feeling

00:07:13.666 --> 00:07:15.501
when he looked at me...

00:07:17.403 --> 00:07:18.337
And...

00:07:19.806 --> 00:07:22.642
I looked at him and he put
his head down on the table.

00:07:27.747 --> 00:07:32.018
I thought I was gonna die,
I couldn't believe it.

00:07:35.321 --> 00:07:39.258
And they stood him up and they removed him

00:07:39.258 --> 00:07:42.395
from the courtroom and I
told him that I loved him

00:07:42.395 --> 00:07:45.665
and he looked back at me
and he said, I love you.

00:07:45.665 --> 00:07:49.168
And I think there was a picture of that,

00:07:49.168 --> 00:07:52.939
what I was feeling, because
I just couldn't believe it,

00:07:52.939 --> 00:07:54.774
absolutely couldn't believe it.

00:08:03.649 --> 00:08:04.784
I had to sell our home,

00:08:04.784 --> 00:08:07.820
I had to find somewhere to
move, I had lost my job.

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:12.124
It was me and my sons,
period, that was it.

00:08:12.124 --> 00:08:15.161
I had not a lot of support.

00:08:16.863 --> 00:08:19.398
Clarence's family was devastated.

00:08:19.398 --> 00:08:21.367
I couldn't go to my family.

00:08:22.702 --> 00:08:27.173
So I had to concentrate on
getting myself together first.

00:08:27.173 --> 00:08:32.078
And I did that, and then after that,

00:08:33.212 --> 00:08:37.416
I decided, first of all,
Clarence needs new attorneys

00:08:37.416 --> 00:08:41.487
and we need a private investigator.

00:08:41.487 --> 00:08:45.992
Clarence is not guilty and I
was gonna somehow prove that.

00:08:47.693 --> 00:08:50.530
♪ It's not my home ♪

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:52.398
♪ But here I am ♪

00:08:52.398 --> 00:08:54.867
♪ Stealin' all your wishes ♪

00:08:54.867 --> 00:08:57.803
♪ Better grab them while you can ♪

00:08:57.803 --> 00:08:59.805
♪ I've been wrong ♪

00:08:59.805 --> 00:09:00.640
Hi, Mom.

00:09:03.075 --> 00:09:05.578
I love you and I miss you.

00:09:05.578 --> 00:09:09.215
I made a vow to her that
I would find the truth,

00:09:09.215 --> 00:09:13.252
find who did this to her,
if it took me forever.

00:09:16.522 --> 00:09:17.356
Bye, Grandma.

00:09:18.491 --> 00:09:22.762
♪ Countin' stars, there
must be a million ♪

00:09:22.762 --> 00:09:25.565
♪ I could see them through the bars ♪

00:09:25.565 --> 00:09:28.167
♪ I'm alone ♪

00:09:28.167 --> 00:09:31.003
♪ But it's all right ♪

00:09:31.003 --> 00:09:35.141
♪ I've got a dream to make it right ♪

00:09:35.141 --> 00:09:38.311
♪ Healin' on light ♪

00:09:38.311 --> 00:09:43.316
- So she walked up to my door
and my husband answered it.

00:09:43.349 --> 00:09:45.751
She ask if she could come in.

00:09:45.751 --> 00:09:48.721
- She first turned away from
me when I come to the door,

00:09:48.721 --> 00:09:50.790
and then immediately turned back to me

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:53.993
and hugged me and cried.

00:09:53.993 --> 00:09:57.663
And I thought, I've broken the ice now.

00:09:57.663 --> 00:09:59.565
Now we can talk.

00:09:59.565 --> 00:10:04.570
- I was glad that you came
over and tried talkin' to me.

00:10:04.570 --> 00:10:06.572
I really, I don't know what I was thinkin'

00:10:06.572 --> 00:10:10.810
at that point in time, but
I know that I had missed her

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:13.145
and I loved her and I wanted
to see if her and my nephews

00:10:13.145 --> 00:10:17.416
were all right, so I chose
to talk to her that day.

00:10:18.584 --> 00:10:20.820
And I'm glad I did.

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:22.254
Like I said, I've known Clarence

00:10:22.254 --> 00:10:23.656
since I was eight years old.

00:10:24.924 --> 00:10:27.660
So at the same time, when
I believed he was guilty,

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:29.395
there was still a doubt in my mind

00:10:29.395 --> 00:10:32.064
that maybe he did not commit this crime.

00:10:32.064 --> 00:10:34.400
This is Brooke's first grade picture.

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:38.371
I told my sister, whatever
you need, I'll do, interviews,

00:10:38.371 --> 00:10:43.376
whatever we need to do to try
and get to the bottom of this.

00:10:43.409 --> 00:10:45.111
(traffic passing)

00:10:45.111 --> 00:10:47.580
- When a person accepts Christ the Savior,

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:51.283
they become born again,
the salvation takes place.

00:10:51.283 --> 00:10:53.819
That they're saved, they're Christian.

00:10:53.819 --> 00:10:56.322
When that happens, the
Holy Spirit's in them

00:10:56.322 --> 00:10:58.791
and he starts guiding you to all truth.

00:10:58.791 --> 00:11:02.662
- I just wanted to be in her life again.

00:11:02.662 --> 00:11:04.630
I'm her aunt and I loved her.

00:11:04.630 --> 00:11:07.800
And I wanted to be there for my sister.

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:09.168
I know that she was...

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:15.374
devastated by all of this, as we all have.

00:11:16.809 --> 00:11:19.445
(light music)

00:11:19.445 --> 00:11:22.682
So what happened was Brooke
wanted to come and visit.

00:11:22.682 --> 00:11:25.317
- I always hung out with
my cousins in the garage

00:11:25.317 --> 00:11:30.322
because they're really cool
people you could hangout with.

00:11:30.423 --> 00:11:34.393
- And my son, Brandon,
had this photo gallery

00:11:34.393 --> 00:11:36.662
of his dad out in the garage.

00:11:36.662 --> 00:11:39.565
- And I seen Uncle
Clarence in a family photo

00:11:39.565 --> 00:11:43.936
with all his brothers and I
noticed that he had blue eyes.

00:11:47.239 --> 00:11:51.711
And later, I told Aunt
Mindy that the person

00:11:51.711 --> 00:11:55.614
that killed mamaw didn't have
blue eyes, they had brown.

00:12:02.154 --> 00:12:05.357
Sometimes I feel guilty because

00:12:05.357 --> 00:12:10.362
it was my testimony
that put him in prison,

00:12:10.529 --> 00:12:13.999
but they always tell me
that it's not my fault,

00:12:13.999 --> 00:12:18.971
it's the police department's
fault and the prosecutors.

00:12:19.805 --> 00:12:23.542
- Victim misidentification, bottom line.

00:12:23.542 --> 00:12:24.710
- This is my brother's.

00:12:24.710 --> 00:12:27.346
- I mean, you have to remember,
she was six years old.

00:12:27.346 --> 00:12:32.351
She was beaten, raped, strangled,
sodomized, left for dead.

00:12:35.421 --> 00:12:39.558
And she was just traumatized.

00:12:39.558 --> 00:12:41.994
- I told them it wasn't sure who it was,

00:12:41.994 --> 00:12:45.264
but then they're like,
Your Uncle Clarence did it.

00:12:45.264 --> 00:12:48.167
So I was like, yeah, I guess.

00:13:08.721 --> 00:13:11.056
- I'm Mark Godsey, I'm
a law professor here

00:13:11.056 --> 00:13:12.691
at the University of
Cincinnati College of Law

00:13:12.691 --> 00:13:15.494
and director of the
Ohio Innocence Project.

00:13:15.494 --> 00:13:17.830
Our mission is to look at old cases

00:13:17.830 --> 00:13:21.734
where the inmates have
steadfastly maintained

00:13:21.734 --> 00:13:23.969
their innocence from
the beginning to the end

00:13:23.969 --> 00:13:26.105
and see if there's some sort of evidence,

00:13:26.105 --> 00:13:29.041
new evidence, that might be
able to shed light on it.

00:13:29.041 --> 00:13:31.177
- The brother and the sister
don't really get along.

00:13:31.177 --> 00:13:33.112
- The Innocence Projects use law students

00:13:33.112 --> 00:13:35.548
to investigate the cases of
people who they're innocent

00:13:35.548 --> 00:13:37.483
and look into DNA testing
and other technologies.

00:13:37.483 --> 00:13:40.419
So it's a real hands on
experience for the students.

00:13:40.419 --> 00:13:43.489
- They liked Bill for this
crime from the very start.

00:13:43.489 --> 00:13:47.393
He has priors, assault,
hardcore drug convictions.

00:13:47.393 --> 00:13:48.627
- My name is Michelle Berry.

00:13:48.627 --> 00:13:51.564
I'm a third year student
here at UC Law School.

00:13:51.564 --> 00:13:54.900
And the reason I came to UC Law School

00:13:54.900 --> 00:13:58.003
actually was because of
the Ohio Innocence Project.

00:13:58.003 --> 00:14:01.140
- The simple mission is to

00:14:01.140 --> 00:14:02.708
get innocent people out of prison.

00:14:02.708 --> 00:14:04.109
- One of the greatest
feelings in the world

00:14:04.109 --> 00:14:06.478
is knowin' that you're helpin' someone

00:14:06.478 --> 00:14:08.981
who really needs your help
and is actually innocent.

00:14:08.981 --> 00:14:11.650
- We definitely get tons
and tons of letters.

00:14:11.650 --> 00:14:13.686
Just last year I think we had about 500.

00:14:13.686 --> 00:14:15.487
- You take everyone who's
written in and says,

00:14:15.487 --> 00:14:17.456
I'm innocent, please help me.

00:14:17.456 --> 00:14:19.558
The first thing that
happens if you determine

00:14:19.558 --> 00:14:21.861
whether this is a case where DNA

00:14:21.861 --> 00:14:23.495
could actually prove you innocent

00:14:24.697 --> 00:14:26.932
and it the type of crime where
DNA would have been left.

00:14:26.932 --> 00:14:29.535
75% of those cases, the answer's no.

00:14:29.535 --> 00:14:31.737
- So there's a lot that's
wrong with the case,

00:14:31.737 --> 00:14:34.073
there's not a whole lot
of exculpatory evidence.

00:14:34.073 --> 00:14:35.507
- Some people see me
as a defense attorney.

00:14:35.507 --> 00:14:38.210
I don't see myself as a
defense attorney at all.

00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:39.411
I only go forward, and we only go forward,

00:14:39.411 --> 00:14:41.013
we think the evidence
proves they're innocent.

00:14:41.013 --> 00:14:43.716
- I first apologized
for calling him at home,

00:14:43.716 --> 00:14:45.484
but he was like, Oh, no, no, that's okay.

00:14:45.484 --> 00:14:47.119
- She's very compelling
when you listen to her

00:14:47.119 --> 00:14:49.722
and we ended up having this
hour or two hour conversation

00:14:49.722 --> 00:14:51.056
where she filled me in on everything,

00:14:51.056 --> 00:14:52.458
and I just kept wanting
to hear more and more

00:14:52.458 --> 00:14:53.859
'cause it sounded like such a good case,

00:14:53.859 --> 00:14:55.594
a case where we had a real good chance

00:14:55.594 --> 00:14:56.428
of havin' someone innocent here

00:14:56.428 --> 00:14:58.230
that we could do justice with.

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:01.400
(light music)

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:04.270
The crucial, I think,
investigative part of the case

00:15:04.270 --> 00:15:05.804
was when we had a two page list

00:15:05.804 --> 00:15:07.673
of all the items taken
from the crime scene,

00:15:07.673 --> 00:15:09.375
and most of those had never been tested.

00:15:09.375 --> 00:15:11.277
And we had a limited amount of money,

00:15:11.277 --> 00:15:13.145
we didn't have the money
to test everything,

00:15:13.145 --> 00:15:14.246
so we had to make strategic decisions

00:15:14.246 --> 00:15:15.848
on what was gonna be tested.

00:15:21.887 --> 00:15:24.290
- We had a slam dunk case then and there.

00:15:24.290 --> 00:15:28.560
Evidence that could only have
been left by the true attacker

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:31.130
and completely excluded Mr. Elkins.

00:15:31.130 --> 00:15:33.933
- Before that, there's always some doubt.

00:15:33.933 --> 00:15:36.468
Maybe you're helpin' someone
who's not really innocent,

00:15:36.468 --> 00:15:40.539
but then when you get it
back and you feel vindicated,

00:15:40.539 --> 00:15:42.474
you feel like you've done
something really good

00:15:42.474 --> 00:15:45.244
and now it's just a
matter of going to court

00:15:45.244 --> 00:15:48.781
or getting the prosecutor to
agree and get 'em out of there.

00:15:48.781 --> 00:15:51.283
- So we went forward with
that and made a motion

00:15:51.283 --> 00:15:54.320
that lead to a hearing in March of 2005.

00:15:54.320 --> 00:15:56.121
We got up and we argued to the judge.

00:15:56.121 --> 00:15:57.556
Judge, we know who the true killer is.

00:15:57.556 --> 00:16:00.025
We don't know his name,
but here's his DNA profile

00:16:00.025 --> 00:16:01.961
on the chalkboard, it's
not Clarence Elkins

00:16:01.961 --> 00:16:03.696
and Clarence Elkins should be exonerated.

00:16:03.696 --> 00:16:06.665
And I think on that evidence,

00:16:06.665 --> 00:16:08.934
if you were to play Russian roulette

00:16:08.934 --> 00:16:10.102
and drop yourself in any
courthouse in America,

00:16:10.102 --> 00:16:12.137
you'd win about 99% of the time.

00:16:13.172 --> 00:16:14.707
If you can believe it, we actually lost

00:16:14.707 --> 00:16:16.408
that hearing in July of 2005.

00:16:16.408 --> 00:16:19.144
The judge came down with the
decision denying our motion

00:16:19.144 --> 00:16:20.579
and that Clarence had to spend

00:16:20.579 --> 00:16:22.214
the rest of his life in prison.

00:16:22.214 --> 00:16:26.585
(train horn sounding)
(light music)

00:16:26.585 --> 00:16:30.956
- A new trial at the least was denied?

00:16:30.956 --> 00:16:33.726
(doors slamming)

00:16:37.062 --> 00:16:41.333
There's something wrong with this picture.

00:16:41.333 --> 00:16:44.336
What doesn't these people understand?

00:16:45.270 --> 00:16:50.275
These people are supposed to
be people that uphold the law.

00:16:51.577 --> 00:16:55.080
That's not justice, that's
not what the people deserve,

00:16:55.080 --> 00:16:56.415
that's not what I deserve.

00:16:56.415 --> 00:16:59.151
That's not what my mother in
law and my niece deserves.

00:17:01.487 --> 00:17:04.990
♪ Tell you what was wrong ♪

00:17:07.860 --> 00:17:11.630
- I was totally devastated.

00:17:11.630 --> 00:17:13.265
Sorry.

00:17:13.265 --> 00:17:17.736
I just broke down, I cried and I sobbed

00:17:19.271 --> 00:17:20.305
and I couldn't collect myself.

00:17:20.305 --> 00:17:21.974
I just couldn't believe it.

00:17:21.974 --> 00:17:23.075
What more do that want?

00:17:23.075 --> 00:17:24.510
Do they want us to serve them

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:26.745
who did this on a silver platter to them?

00:17:26.745 --> 00:17:28.647
Well, okay then, that's what we'll do.

00:17:33.052 --> 00:17:35.421
- [Mark] For people who
are innocent in prison,

00:17:35.421 --> 00:17:36.655
the police aren't gonna
investigate their cases,

00:17:36.655 --> 00:17:39.158
because are far as the police
is concerned, they're guilty.

00:17:39.158 --> 00:17:41.960
And so Melinda was really
the detective in this case

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:43.762
and she was going out in the town,

00:17:44.997 --> 00:17:46.598
getting tips and following up on leads.

00:17:46.598 --> 00:17:47.933
And we were all brainstorming

00:17:47.933 --> 00:17:49.668
on who could have done this crime.

00:17:49.668 --> 00:17:53.672
- And then at one point, we
had this list of suspects.

00:17:53.672 --> 00:17:56.208
When the DNA testing
come around that time,

00:17:56.208 --> 00:17:58.077
we really had to start thinkin',

00:17:58.077 --> 00:18:00.145
well, which ones are we gonna have tested?

00:18:00.145 --> 00:18:04.316
Which ones do we want
to try to get DNA from?

00:18:04.316 --> 00:18:05.918
- I remember having several conversations

00:18:05.918 --> 00:18:07.052
with her where she'd say,

00:18:07.052 --> 00:18:08.554
I'm gonna go in and meet
this guy in a bar tonight

00:18:08.554 --> 00:18:10.322
and I'm gonna walk out
with his beer better.

00:18:10.322 --> 00:18:12.858
- And I concentrated on people that I knew

00:18:12.858 --> 00:18:15.761
around the Barberton area
that had a colorful past

00:18:15.761 --> 00:18:18.597
who might be capable
of something like this.

00:18:18.597 --> 00:18:21.633
And I would interject
myself into their lives

00:18:21.633 --> 00:18:25.904
and just follow them, and
the knew me well enough

00:18:25.904 --> 00:18:28.941
as Judy's daughter and April's sister

00:18:28.941 --> 00:18:33.145
that I could talk to them
and they wouldn't suspect

00:18:33.145 --> 00:18:36.515
that I was getting their DNA on the sly.

00:18:37.883 --> 00:18:40.385
And I would get a beer bottle or a glass,

00:18:40.385 --> 00:18:44.089
and I had some of the
bartenders helping me.

00:18:45.090 --> 00:18:47.826
It was pretty sickening to have to pretend

00:18:47.826 --> 00:18:51.163
like you're talking to
these people as a friend

00:18:51.163 --> 00:18:52.764
when you're actually thinking

00:18:52.764 --> 00:18:55.300
is this the person that killed my mom?

00:19:02.141 --> 00:19:05.811
Looking back on it now, if
they ever would have suspected,

00:19:05.811 --> 00:19:08.947
then I just wonder what
could have happened to me.

00:19:08.947 --> 00:19:12.084
But I was focused and I know
that I needed to do this

00:19:12.084 --> 00:19:15.554
because there wasn't anyone
else to do that, so I did it.

00:19:22.895 --> 00:19:25.164
I'm heading to Tonia Brasiel's house,

00:19:25.164 --> 00:19:26.832
the front door, actually.

00:19:26.832 --> 00:19:29.801
- Tonia Brasiel is the woman
who lived two doors down

00:19:29.801 --> 00:19:32.938
and the morning that the crime occurred,

00:19:32.938 --> 00:19:33.872
Brooke Sutton, the little girl,

00:19:33.872 --> 00:19:36.008
woke up and ran two doors down for help.

00:19:36.008 --> 00:19:39.077
- I knocked on her door and
I asked her to take me home,

00:19:39.077 --> 00:19:44.082
that my grandma was dead
and that someone killed her.

00:19:44.449 --> 00:19:46.685
- Tonia Brasiel answers the door,

00:19:46.685 --> 00:19:47.686
instead of doing what you would expect,

00:19:47.686 --> 00:19:50.489
which is call the police,
call an ambulance,

00:19:50.489 --> 00:19:53.892
call her mom, she closes the door.

00:19:53.892 --> 00:19:56.762
- She didn't let my niece in that morning.

00:19:56.762 --> 00:20:00.332
She puts her in her
car and takes her home.

00:20:00.332 --> 00:20:04.102
- And I ask her, I said, Why
did you not go check on my mom?

00:20:04.102 --> 00:20:06.838
And she said that she was scared
someone was still in there

00:20:06.838 --> 00:20:09.107
and that they would hurt
her and the children.

00:20:09.107 --> 00:20:12.544
And I said, Well, did you call
911, did you call the police,

00:20:12.544 --> 00:20:14.112
did you call anyone to get my mom help?

00:20:14.112 --> 00:20:15.514
And she said no.

00:20:15.514 --> 00:20:17.082
- I couldn't understand, I mean,

00:20:17.082 --> 00:20:19.017
a child would come to my door

00:20:19.017 --> 00:20:20.786
in the shape that Brooke was in,

00:20:20.786 --> 00:20:25.324
she was very disheveled
and bloody and injured,

00:20:25.324 --> 00:20:27.392
your first instinct would
be to take that child in

00:20:27.392 --> 00:20:30.329
and protect her, and then call the police.

00:20:30.329 --> 00:20:33.165
- She became, Tonia Brasiel,
the woman two doors down,

00:20:33.165 --> 00:20:35.400
the key witness against Clarence Elkins,

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:36.768
in addition to the little girl,

00:20:36.768 --> 00:20:39.104
because her testimony at trial was,

00:20:39.104 --> 00:20:40.672
When the little girl knocked on my door,

00:20:40.672 --> 00:20:41.773
she was screaming over
and over again that,

00:20:41.773 --> 00:20:43.542
It was my Uncle Clarence,
it was my Uncle Clarence,

00:20:43.542 --> 00:20:45.010
I'm positive it was my Uncle Clarence!

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:46.945
And so you actually had
an adult corroborating

00:20:46.945 --> 00:20:49.248
and backing up this weak testimony
from a six year old girl.

00:20:49.248 --> 00:20:50.983
- I just kept going
back to that and I kept,

00:20:50.983 --> 00:20:52.251
why would somebody do that?

00:20:52.251 --> 00:20:54.386
What would be the reason?

00:20:54.386 --> 00:20:56.555
Is there something they
were trying to hide?

00:20:56.555 --> 00:20:59.057
(light music)

00:21:01.426 --> 00:21:03.762
I'd come across a newspaper article

00:21:03.762 --> 00:21:05.764
that featured her name with Earl Mann,

00:21:05.764 --> 00:21:08.233
who was her common law husband,

00:21:08.233 --> 00:21:09.835
and that they were both being charged

00:21:09.835 --> 00:21:12.838
for rape of three children.

00:21:12.838 --> 00:21:15.107
- Specifically, Earl Mann was convicted

00:21:15.107 --> 00:21:17.409
and Tonia Brasiel was convicted
for aiding and abetting him.

00:21:17.409 --> 00:21:18.310
And these were anal rapes,

00:21:18.310 --> 00:21:20.479
which matched what had
happened in this crime.

00:21:20.479 --> 00:21:24.483
- And I thought, my god, the possibility

00:21:24.483 --> 00:21:29.488
that he's a child rapist, there
was some kind of light bulb

00:21:31.256 --> 00:21:34.693
that went off that said to me,
you need to look at this guy.

00:21:34.693 --> 00:21:36.061
- And looking at the records,

00:21:36.061 --> 00:21:37.296
which you could all see on the internet,

00:21:37.296 --> 00:21:40.032
he got out of prison on June 2nd

00:21:40.032 --> 00:21:42.868
and then was picked up again
for another crime on June 15th,

00:21:42.868 --> 00:21:45.737
and the murders occurred and
the rapes occurred on June 7th.

00:21:45.737 --> 00:21:48.740
So he was out of a window
for about 13 days there,

00:21:48.740 --> 00:21:51.443
staying at that house where
his common law wife was.

00:21:51.443 --> 00:21:53.345
So it all just fit and immediately

00:21:53.345 --> 00:21:54.913
he becomes the top suspect.

00:21:54.913 --> 00:21:56.114
- He had close proximity,

00:21:56.114 --> 00:21:59.518
it's just second steps away
from my mom's front door.

00:21:59.518 --> 00:22:04.523
I'm thinking that he had
quick access to my mom's house

00:22:04.956 --> 00:22:08.694
and quick access back home
without being detected.

00:22:08.694 --> 00:22:11.863
Knowing that he lived here
and what his background was,

00:22:11.863 --> 00:22:13.632
I'm really thinking that he's the person

00:22:13.632 --> 00:22:16.001
that murdered and raped my mom

00:22:16.001 --> 00:22:18.637
and attacked and raped my niece.

00:22:18.637 --> 00:22:21.606
- Also on the computer,
you can type in Earl Mann,

00:22:21.606 --> 00:22:24.376
on the websites we go to,
and see where he's in prison

00:22:24.376 --> 00:22:25.644
for raping these little girls.

00:22:25.644 --> 00:22:29.047
And low and behold, he's in
prison with Clarence Elkins.

00:22:29.047 --> 00:22:31.550
(background chattering)

00:22:31.550 --> 00:22:34.052
(light music)

00:22:39.658 --> 00:22:42.294
- My wife Melinda told me of this suspect

00:22:42.294 --> 00:22:46.298
that she had in mind, and
there was several ways

00:22:46.298 --> 00:22:48.834
I thought about retrieving DNA.

00:22:48.834 --> 00:22:52.003
I didn't wanna point a finger
at somebody I wasn't sure of.

00:22:53.605 --> 00:22:57.275
Once and for all, the opportunity arose.

00:22:57.275 --> 00:23:00.512
He had smoked a cigarette,
sittin' there at the table,

00:23:00.512 --> 00:23:05.517
so there was a single
cigarette butt in this ashtray.

00:23:06.585 --> 00:23:09.287
And it was just the perfect time.

00:23:09.287 --> 00:23:14.292
I had some tissue in my
pocket and I took the tissue,

00:23:14.626 --> 00:23:19.631
reached into the ashtray with
the tissue without touching it

00:23:20.866 --> 00:23:24.269
and wrapped it up, put it in plastic,

00:23:24.269 --> 00:23:26.938
and mailed it out in a envelope.

00:23:34.012 --> 00:23:37.983
- It was just a shock,
and everybody in my office

00:23:37.983 --> 00:23:41.353
could hear me saying, Oh, thank god.

00:23:41.353 --> 00:23:43.655
Oh my god, what?

00:23:43.655 --> 00:23:45.824
- If we had a slam dunk case before,

00:23:45.824 --> 00:23:49.327
we now have a 360 dunk from the foul line

00:23:49.327 --> 00:23:52.097
that breaks the backboard, this was it.

00:23:52.097 --> 00:23:56.768
- All's I could say was it
had to be divine intervention.

00:23:56.768 --> 00:24:01.773
This person was put in my path,
so to speak, for a reason.

00:24:10.849 --> 00:24:12.651
I thank the Lord everyday for it.

00:24:19.224 --> 00:24:22.127
- We got the results back from Earl Mann,

00:24:22.127 --> 00:24:23.895
showing that the true
killer was Earl Mann.

00:24:23.895 --> 00:24:26.932
And having gone through
what we already went through

00:24:26.932 --> 00:24:30.268
after we'd previously proven
that Clarence was innocent,

00:24:30.268 --> 00:24:32.270
and we saw that there's nothing we can do

00:24:32.270 --> 00:24:33.638
to change the mind of these prosecutors,

00:24:33.638 --> 00:24:36.274
there's nothing we can do to
change the mind of the judge,

00:24:36.274 --> 00:24:38.310
we decided we needed some help,

00:24:38.310 --> 00:24:43.315
we needed to do something
unusual to turn the tide.

00:24:43.715 --> 00:24:46.284
- [Narrator] Mark Godsey
decides to reach out

00:24:46.284 --> 00:24:48.887
to the state's top law
enforcement official,

00:24:48.887 --> 00:24:51.890
Attorney General Jim Petro.

00:24:51.890 --> 00:24:54.826
- And when I had the
opportunity to hear the story

00:24:54.826 --> 00:24:57.229
of Clarence Elkins, I
found it very compelling.

00:24:57.229 --> 00:24:59.664
This looks like this guy
could really be innocent.

00:24:59.664 --> 00:25:02.534
We have to do all that we can to assist

00:25:02.534 --> 00:25:04.169
if in fact there's
evidence to suggest that.

00:25:04.169 --> 00:25:07.105
- It wasn't a matter of one in hundred,

00:25:07.105 --> 00:25:09.274
one in a thousand, one in a
million, one in two billion,

00:25:09.274 --> 00:25:13.612
it was absolutely 100%
not Clarence Elkins.

00:25:13.612 --> 00:25:15.780
- I'd asked my chief deputy
for criminal justice,

00:25:15.780 --> 00:25:19.251
Jim Canepa, to please
communicate personally,

00:25:19.251 --> 00:25:21.453
sit down and have a
discussion with the prosecutor

00:25:21.453 --> 00:25:23.722
or with the prosecutor's chief deputy.

00:25:23.722 --> 00:25:26.391
And we found it very
difficult to arrange that.

00:25:26.391 --> 00:25:28.093
- I actually traveled to Akron

00:25:28.093 --> 00:25:29.728
to meet with the county prosecutor,

00:25:29.728 --> 00:25:32.330
tried to speak to her both
in person and by phone,

00:25:32.330 --> 00:25:37.335
and for whatever reasons,
that wasn't accommodated.

00:25:37.435 --> 00:25:39.371
- Maybe they thought that
we were sticking our nose in

00:25:39.371 --> 00:25:42.440
where it didn't belong, but
that's really not the case.

00:25:42.440 --> 00:25:46.878
We do have a good deal of
criminal investigative authority

00:25:46.878 --> 00:25:49.948
when invited by local
law enforcement agencies.

00:25:49.948 --> 00:25:51.750
And this is pretty compelling,

00:25:51.750 --> 00:25:53.652
you really oughta be looking at this,

00:25:53.652 --> 00:25:57.122
because no one wants to sit
back and let a guy sit in jail

00:25:57.122 --> 00:25:59.291
when he might in fact really be innocent.

00:25:59.291 --> 00:26:00.892
- The DNA evidence connecting Earl Mann

00:26:00.892 --> 00:26:03.595
was very powerful originally and there was

00:26:03.595 --> 00:26:05.964
a less than one in 4,000 chance
that it was someone else,

00:26:05.964 --> 00:26:07.032
which is very, very powerful.

00:26:07.032 --> 00:26:08.934
But as the prosecution
argued in their attempts

00:26:08.934 --> 00:26:11.970
to protect Earl Mann and
avoid admitting a mistake

00:26:11.970 --> 00:26:14.239
had been made with Clarence
Elkins, they said, well,

00:26:14.239 --> 00:26:16.274
that's several hundred
men in the state of Ohio.

00:26:16.274 --> 00:26:17.108
Of course, if you took those

00:26:17.108 --> 00:26:18.610
several hundred men
and put them in a room,

00:26:18.610 --> 00:26:19.945
some of them would be two year old babies

00:26:19.945 --> 00:26:21.413
and some of them would be 98 year old men,

00:26:21.413 --> 00:26:23.481
some of them would be African
American and different races.

00:26:23.481 --> 00:26:25.417
And the number of these other men in Ohio

00:26:25.417 --> 00:26:27.953
that had this DNA profile
who are convicted rapists,

00:26:27.953 --> 00:26:29.454
child rapists, who live two doors down

00:26:29.454 --> 00:26:30.722
from the crime scene would be zero.

00:26:30.722 --> 00:26:34.025
- [Crowd] Free Clarence
now, free Clarence now!

00:26:34.025 --> 00:26:37.596
Free Clarence now, free Clarence now!

00:26:37.596 --> 00:26:42.601
Free Clarence now, free
Clarence now, free Clarence now!

00:26:42.834 --> 00:26:44.469
- This is not about myself.

00:26:47.072 --> 00:26:51.309
This is for my mother in law and my niece.

00:26:53.612 --> 00:26:55.747
And for the public in general.

00:26:58.149 --> 00:27:00.251
I think there's a lesson to be learned

00:27:00.251 --> 00:27:04.122
for a lot of people in this situation.

00:27:05.590 --> 00:27:08.693
Injustices can happen,
as they happened to me,

00:27:10.128 --> 00:27:13.698
and I wouldn't ever want that
to happen to anyone else.

00:27:13.698 --> 00:27:17.202
I want justice for everyone.

00:27:19.137 --> 00:27:23.875
And the person that
committed this crime...

00:27:25.710 --> 00:27:27.112
be held accountable.

00:27:28.446 --> 00:27:30.949
(light music)

00:27:36.988 --> 00:27:38.223
- I think the real question here is,

00:27:38.223 --> 00:27:41.159
when you absolutely know
the person is innocent,

00:27:41.159 --> 00:27:43.662
what can you do to speed that process up?

00:27:43.662 --> 00:27:46.097
The guy is innocent, what can we do

00:27:46.097 --> 00:27:47.699
to get him out before Christmas?

00:27:56.141 --> 00:27:57.909
- Well, right now we're getting ready

00:27:57.909 --> 00:28:01.913
to go to the attorney
general's press conference,

00:28:01.913 --> 00:28:05.717
so I think this would be
the best Christmas present

00:28:05.717 --> 00:28:08.086
that any of us could ever ask for.

00:28:08.086 --> 00:28:10.655
I'm feelin' it now, I'm just...

00:28:13.091 --> 00:28:14.626
nervous.

00:28:14.626 --> 00:28:16.428
- A few minutes before our
press conference starts,

00:28:16.428 --> 00:28:20.065
we get a call from them
telling us that they've agreed,

00:28:20.065 --> 00:28:20.899
that they're gonna be announcing it there

00:28:20.899 --> 00:28:23.034
that Clarence is innocent
and he's gonna be released.

00:28:23.034 --> 00:28:25.070
Jim Petro, the attorney
general, just came in

00:28:25.070 --> 00:28:27.305
and announced that Summit County

00:28:27.305 --> 00:28:29.574
is holding a press conference
at the same time as ours

00:28:29.574 --> 00:28:31.576
and they're gonna announce that
they're dropping all charges

00:28:31.576 --> 00:28:33.545
against Clarence and
his immediate release.

00:28:33.545 --> 00:28:36.381
(Melinda sobbing)

00:28:42.153 --> 00:28:44.222
Apparently what had
happened, we learned later,

00:28:44.222 --> 00:28:46.624
is that they had started
interrogating Earl Mann

00:28:46.624 --> 00:28:48.927
after they got the pubic hair match.

00:28:48.927 --> 00:28:51.029
And he had made several
incriminating statements.

00:28:51.029 --> 00:28:53.398
They'd finally come around to the view

00:28:53.398 --> 00:28:54.466
that Clarence was innocent.

00:28:54.466 --> 00:28:57.068
- When we commenced to call you in today

00:28:57.068 --> 00:29:00.071
to make another announcement,
we were announcing

00:29:00.071 --> 00:29:03.308
the completion of scientific
testing by criminologists.

00:29:03.308 --> 00:29:05.610
That information was provided yesterday

00:29:05.610 --> 00:29:08.813
to the prosecutor's office,
and I guess, I'm not tryin'

00:29:08.813 --> 00:29:10.215
to steal their announcement,
but they're making

00:29:10.215 --> 00:29:13.284
a comparable announcement
in Akron at 11:00.

00:29:13.284 --> 00:29:15.153
- Press release, Summit County prosecutor

00:29:15.153 --> 00:29:17.355
moves to free man
convicted in slaying rape.

00:29:17.355 --> 00:29:19.657
Summit County prosecutor
Sherri Bevan Walsh

00:29:19.657 --> 00:29:21.626
today filed a motion to
clear Clarence Elkins,

00:29:21.626 --> 00:29:24.429
who was convicted in 1999 of
the murder of his mother in law

00:29:24.429 --> 00:29:26.197
and the rape of his six year old niece.

00:29:26.197 --> 00:29:28.399
- Yeah, he's been exonerated,
they dropped all the charges

00:29:28.399 --> 00:29:30.735
and he's getting out late this afternoon.

00:29:30.735 --> 00:29:33.671
- If we can get the entry from the judge.

00:29:33.671 --> 00:29:35.240
I was tryin' to figure out a way

00:29:35.240 --> 00:29:38.243
to get a phone call into
Clarence, because at that point,

00:29:38.243 --> 00:29:41.079
he was being protected
in protective custody.

00:29:41.079 --> 00:29:43.882
But I got right through
and I was really excited

00:29:43.882 --> 00:29:45.583
that she was gonna be
able to talk to Clarence

00:29:45.583 --> 00:29:47.018
and give him the news herself.

00:29:47.018 --> 00:29:48.453
Okay, very good.

00:29:48.453 --> 00:29:49.487
- [Man] Okay, well I need to run because--

00:29:49.487 --> 00:29:50.889
- It's Nannette?

00:29:50.889 --> 00:29:52.190
Hey, Clarence?

00:29:52.190 --> 00:29:53.525
What are you doin', honey?

00:29:55.059 --> 00:29:56.928
You okay?

00:29:56.928 --> 00:29:58.930
You ready to come home?

00:29:58.930 --> 00:30:00.231
Then get your stuff packed, honey,

00:30:00.231 --> 00:30:01.933
'cause you're comin' home today.

00:30:03.835 --> 00:30:05.970
- [Clarence] Well, God
bless all of you guys.

00:30:05.970 --> 00:30:07.705
You guys are amazing.

00:30:08.840 --> 00:30:10.074
- [Mark] You're amazing, Clarence.

00:30:10.074 --> 00:30:12.577
- [Clarence] Oh my god,
I'm just so grateful.

00:30:13.511 --> 00:30:14.345
- [Mark] Hey Clarence?

00:30:14.345 --> 00:30:15.180
- [Clarence] What?

00:30:15.180 --> 00:30:16.247
- Have you known that
this has been going on?

00:30:16.247 --> 00:30:19.417
We've all been frantic
for the past 24 hours.

00:30:19.417 --> 00:30:20.385
- [Clarence] I thought maybe

00:30:20.385 --> 00:30:25.390
it was gonna go a little while
longer yet, but this is it.

00:30:28.226 --> 00:30:30.728
(light music)

00:30:33.298 --> 00:30:35.466
- Nobody's gonna be allowed
past this flagpole, please.

00:30:35.466 --> 00:30:37.268
- Not past the flagpole.

00:30:37.268 --> 00:30:39.270
- [Woman] Don't worry,
I won't get in the shot.

00:30:39.270 --> 00:30:40.338
- [Man] Hey, Clarence!

00:30:40.338 --> 00:30:42.941
- Thank God, thank God.

00:30:42.941 --> 00:30:46.110
I'm very proud of everyone
that stepped forward

00:30:46.110 --> 00:30:47.846
on my behalf for justice.

00:30:47.846 --> 00:30:48.713
- [Woman] What are you gonna do?

00:30:48.713 --> 00:30:50.582
- [Man] Pinch yourself?

00:30:50.582 --> 00:30:54.853
- I don't know, one day at a time,

00:30:54.853 --> 00:30:57.956
slow and easy, putting
my life back together.

00:30:57.956 --> 00:30:58.790
Freedom!

00:31:00.391 --> 00:31:01.759
- [Woman] How are you
gonna spend Christmas?

00:31:01.759 --> 00:31:03.294
- [Clarence] With my family.

00:31:03.294 --> 00:31:04.395
- [Man] What do you want for Christmas?

00:31:04.395 --> 00:31:06.731
- I got what I wanted for Christmas,

00:31:06.731 --> 00:31:09.067
my life back with my family.

00:31:10.869 --> 00:31:13.805
♪ All the chains, they can rattle ♪

00:31:13.805 --> 00:31:18.776
♪ Won't scare me away from you ♪

00:31:21.379 --> 00:31:23.615
♪ And all the vainest attempts ♪

00:31:23.615 --> 00:31:28.353
♪ Just leave snide hesitation words ♪

00:31:28.353 --> 00:31:30.555
- This is Uncle Woody's, it's a bar

00:31:30.555 --> 00:31:32.390
right across the street
from the law school

00:31:32.390 --> 00:31:35.326
where it's the local watering
hole for the law students.

00:31:35.326 --> 00:31:36.728
They come to celebrate things,

00:31:36.728 --> 00:31:39.130
so here today celebrating
the Clarence Elkins case.

00:31:39.130 --> 00:31:39.964
This is the first time

00:31:39.964 --> 00:31:41.833
we've gotten together since he got out.

00:31:41.833 --> 00:31:44.168
- It's an inspiration that I know

00:31:44.168 --> 00:31:46.704
that I'm gonna carry with
me throughout my career.

00:31:46.704 --> 00:31:49.407
Knowing that I'm picking up these skills

00:31:49.407 --> 00:31:52.744
and gaining this knowledge
and I'm able to use that

00:31:52.744 --> 00:31:55.546
to help people who
can't do it on their own

00:31:55.546 --> 00:31:57.782
and have no other resort,

00:31:57.782 --> 00:31:59.784
I just know that's what I'm meant to do.

00:31:59.784 --> 00:32:03.354
- Do I think the justice
system is this beautiful thing

00:32:03.354 --> 00:32:05.356
that we learn about in sixth
grade social studies class?

00:32:05.356 --> 00:32:08.226
Absolutely not, but do
I believe in justice?

00:32:08.226 --> 00:32:09.494
Absolutely.

00:32:09.494 --> 00:32:12.697
- [Crowd] Free Clarence
now, free Clarence now!

00:32:12.697 --> 00:32:14.532
- The criminal justice
system isn't perfect,

00:32:14.532 --> 00:32:16.167
but if you fight hard enough

00:32:16.167 --> 00:32:18.102
and you have a lot of people on your side,

00:32:18.102 --> 00:32:20.071
you can eventually win.

00:32:20.071 --> 00:32:22.907
- All right guys, let's have
a toast to all the hard work

00:32:22.907 --> 00:32:23.808
that all of you guys have done,

00:32:23.808 --> 00:32:25.710
not only in the Elkins case,
but all the other cases.

00:32:25.710 --> 00:32:27.111
A great result, and to Clarence.

00:32:27.111 --> 00:32:28.112
- [All] To Clarence!

00:32:29.147 --> 00:32:32.150
(cheering)

00:32:32.150 --> 00:32:32.984
(light music)

00:32:32.984 --> 00:32:35.186
♪ Gonna sit by my window ♪

00:32:35.186 --> 00:32:39.590
♪ I'll just watch the leaves blow away ♪

00:32:39.590 --> 00:32:41.926
- This here road, Irish
Road, is where I used to live

00:32:41.926 --> 00:32:45.196
in Magnolia, Ohio, seven
and a half years ago.

00:32:45.196 --> 00:32:48.166
♪ There's so much that you can take ♪

00:32:48.166 --> 00:32:51.869
♪ Inside yourself before you break ♪

00:32:51.869 --> 00:32:54.806
I had, oh, approximately four acres.

00:32:56.074 --> 00:32:58.009
Used to hunt in all these woods here.

00:32:58.009 --> 00:33:00.845
♪ There's no place for castaways ♪

00:33:00.845 --> 00:33:04.549
♪ Like you and I to call home ♪

00:33:04.549 --> 00:33:08.386
This just brings back a lot
of memories, being here.

00:33:08.386 --> 00:33:13.391
We had a lot of good times
here and this was home.

00:33:14.058 --> 00:33:16.127
This was home seven and a half years ago.

00:33:17.996 --> 00:33:20.965
And it was all taken in an instant.

00:33:23.501 --> 00:33:28.306
It's very difficult, very sad at times.

00:33:30.441 --> 00:33:32.377
It's not like it used to be, but...

00:33:33.578 --> 00:33:36.447
at the same time, I gotta move on.

00:33:39.083 --> 00:33:43.121
And that's our goal, is to move on

00:33:43.121 --> 00:33:45.256
and try to rebuild our lives.

00:33:50.161 --> 00:33:53.698
- The really hard part for
me, I'll speak for myself,

00:33:53.698 --> 00:33:56.801
I wasn't expecting
through all the happiness

00:33:56.801 --> 00:34:01.005
and the sense of accomplishment,
through all of that,

00:34:01.005 --> 00:34:05.343
I was not expecting this rock of...

00:34:07.111 --> 00:34:09.580
sadness to come over me as it did...

00:34:10.515 --> 00:34:11.783
for my mom.

00:34:13.651 --> 00:34:17.488
I'm now starting that grieving
process that I never got

00:34:17.488 --> 00:34:22.493
to do and it's really
hit me extremely hard.

00:34:24.162 --> 00:34:29.167
My reaction has been
to go inside of myself,

00:34:29.567 --> 00:34:32.470
because I've only had myself
to rely on, pretty much.

00:34:32.470 --> 00:34:36.808
I've made it that way only
because I had to put up a wall.

00:34:36.808 --> 00:34:40.812
And that wall has doubled for me.

00:34:40.812 --> 00:34:45.817
I feel very disconnected from everyone.

00:34:51.589 --> 00:34:54.392
- [Narrator] Shortly
after his exoneration,

00:34:54.392 --> 00:34:56.961
Clarence and Melinda become estranged.

00:34:58.463 --> 00:35:03.434
- The hardest part is my wife is Melinda,

00:35:03.768 --> 00:35:08.773
who's been so amazing in her
efforts to get to the truth

00:35:14.212 --> 00:35:16.681
and find out who did this.

00:35:20.751 --> 00:35:25.756
So she is not finished at this time.

00:35:25.790 --> 00:35:29.994
She's still in the
fight mode, so to speak.

00:35:32.663 --> 00:35:36.200
So it's been very rough on us.

00:35:43.040 --> 00:35:47.578
She's havin' a hard time
adjusting to me, I guess.

00:35:47.578 --> 00:35:50.948
I don't know, I really don't know.

00:35:50.948 --> 00:35:52.283
That's the hardest thing

00:35:52.283 --> 00:35:57.288
I'm going through right
now and very painful.

00:36:00.658 --> 00:36:02.994
It's not what I want at all.

00:36:07.064 --> 00:36:09.834
That's about all I can
say right now about that.

00:36:11.669 --> 00:36:14.105
- [Narrator] With nowhere else to go,

00:36:14.105 --> 00:36:16.274
Clarence moves back to his parent's home.

00:36:17.141 --> 00:36:20.845
- Yep, home for Christmas,
home for the New Years.

00:36:20.845 --> 00:36:22.380
Start off the new year good.

00:36:24.682 --> 00:36:26.350
Everything's changed.

00:36:26.350 --> 00:36:29.086
(knocking)

00:36:29.086 --> 00:36:33.324
And just tryin' to rebuild my life.

00:36:33.324 --> 00:36:37.929
That's a major, major task in itself.

00:36:41.432 --> 00:36:42.867
It's great to be home.

00:36:44.502 --> 00:36:47.438
I'm just hoping for a good ending.

00:36:48.573 --> 00:36:53.578
That me and my wife
can be husband and wife

00:36:55.413 --> 00:36:58.683
and have some sort of
normalcy in our lives.

00:36:59.917 --> 00:37:02.954
We're just gonna let time take its course

00:37:02.954 --> 00:37:07.458
and hopefully we will recover one day

00:37:07.458 --> 00:37:09.727
and rebuild our lives as a family.

00:37:15.433 --> 00:37:17.935
(light music)

00:37:25.476 --> 00:37:27.311
- Many people have argued to me

00:37:27.311 --> 00:37:30.548
that all these wrongful convictions

00:37:30.548 --> 00:37:31.882
and ultimate exonerations prove

00:37:31.882 --> 00:37:33.851
is that the system corrects itself.

00:37:33.851 --> 00:37:37.054
And if you take any time
to study this issue at all,

00:37:37.054 --> 00:37:38.222
this phenomenon, you'll see that

00:37:38.222 --> 00:37:39.490
that's just a ridiculous statement.

00:37:39.490 --> 00:37:41.392
Look at what we had to do in this case.

00:37:41.392 --> 00:37:43.828
We had to fight tooth
and nail for everything.

00:37:43.828 --> 00:37:45.796
They refused to admit that
a mistake had been made

00:37:45.796 --> 00:37:47.231
and we had to keep going and doing more.

00:37:47.231 --> 00:37:49.967
This was the system fighting
back and trying to stop us.

00:37:49.967 --> 00:37:51.402
And all the other cases we're litigating

00:37:51.402 --> 00:37:53.037
across the state of Ohio right now,

00:37:53.037 --> 00:37:55.973
all we're doing is seeking
to get the DNA evidence.

00:37:55.973 --> 00:37:57.208
And then you have to get the court,

00:37:57.208 --> 00:37:58.976
or the prosecutor, to
agree to let you test it,

00:37:58.976 --> 00:38:00.444
and most of the time they
won't let you do that.

00:38:00.444 --> 00:38:02.380
And then if they still
refuse to let you out

00:38:02.380 --> 00:38:04.115
after you prove you're innocent,
you have to be lucky enough

00:38:04.115 --> 00:38:05.616
to have someone like Earl
Mann, the true killer,

00:38:05.616 --> 00:38:08.185
move into your pod with you
and drop a cigarette butt

00:38:08.185 --> 00:38:09.287
in front of you so you can pick it up.

00:38:09.287 --> 00:38:11.255
So it's not the system
correcting itself at all,

00:38:11.255 --> 00:38:13.090
it's the system doing everything it can

00:38:13.090 --> 00:38:15.526
to stop this sort of thing,
and then when it does happen

00:38:15.526 --> 00:38:17.962
and we do break through
all the barriers, saying,

00:38:17.962 --> 00:38:20.531
oh, this is an aberration,
it doesn't happen very often

00:38:20.531 --> 00:38:23.000
and this just proves the
system corrects itself.

00:38:23.000 --> 00:38:25.870
That's a ridiculous statement.

00:38:25.870 --> 00:38:28.372
(light music)

00:38:32.476 --> 00:38:35.780
(background chattering)

00:38:49.393 --> 00:38:53.964
- Well, after Clarence was
released on December 15, 2005,

00:38:53.964 --> 00:38:58.969
we expected a quick
indictment of Earl Mann.

00:38:59.236 --> 00:39:02.173
That didn't come, so for
the last year and a half,

00:39:02.173 --> 00:39:04.575
as I understand it, the prosecution

00:39:04.575 --> 00:39:07.445
has been investigating this case.

00:39:07.445 --> 00:39:11.649
And today, which is July 11th,

00:39:11.649 --> 00:39:15.453
they are going to formally
charge him, arraign him today.

00:39:15.453 --> 00:39:19.323
I brought a picture of my
mom, and today is for her.

00:39:20.358 --> 00:39:22.193
I don't know what my
reaction is going to be.

00:39:22.193 --> 00:39:25.463
I know that yesterday it felt like

00:39:25.463 --> 00:39:27.331
today was going to be Christmas,

00:39:27.331 --> 00:39:32.303
and today I'm a little
nervous, a little angry.

00:39:32.303 --> 00:39:33.938
I don't know what my reaction's gonna be,

00:39:33.938 --> 00:39:38.642
but I am happy that it's finally
come to fruition on this.

00:39:40.244 --> 00:39:43.514
I just can't wait until
they formally charge him.

00:39:43.514 --> 00:39:48.519
It's just gonna be a big
amount of pressure off of me,

00:39:48.519 --> 00:39:52.456
because this is for my mom and
I vowed to do that for her,

00:39:52.456 --> 00:39:54.258
and here we are today.

00:39:55.359 --> 00:39:58.696
(background chattering)

00:40:25.556 --> 00:40:28.159
Seeing him in the courtroom was difficult,

00:40:28.159 --> 00:40:30.528
but I felt good about it.

00:40:30.528 --> 00:40:34.398
But I have a lot of confidence
that things are gonna go

00:40:34.398 --> 00:40:35.499
the way they should this time.

00:40:35.499 --> 00:40:36.333
- [Woman] You're gonna follow

00:40:36.333 --> 00:40:37.401
this thing all the way through?

00:40:37.401 --> 00:40:39.603
- All the way through, I'll be here

00:40:39.603 --> 00:40:42.139
every time that he's in that courtroom.

00:40:42.139 --> 00:40:44.308
- [Woman] I see you're
moving on with your life.

00:40:44.308 --> 00:40:46.844
- Yes, this is my fiance, Patrick.

00:40:47.845 --> 00:40:49.947
Clarence and I are divorced now

00:40:49.947 --> 00:40:53.517
and he is in a relationship
and I'm engaged.

00:40:53.517 --> 00:40:55.386
And we're just tryin' to pick up

00:40:55.386 --> 00:40:59.390
the pieces again and live life.

00:40:59.390 --> 00:41:02.993
A lot of misconception that
the public and the media have

00:41:02.993 --> 00:41:07.998
is that I wasn't doing this
for the sake of my marriage,

00:41:09.133 --> 00:41:11.735
I was doing it because it
was the right thing to do.

00:41:11.735 --> 00:41:16.006
I couldn't stand the thought
of him sitting in prison

00:41:16.006 --> 00:41:17.341
for something he didn't do.

00:41:20.411 --> 00:41:24.615
The vow that I made to my
mom was the main objective.

00:41:24.615 --> 00:41:28.352
And just the feelings of husband and wife

00:41:29.320 --> 00:41:33.090
were somehow buried
underneath all the pain

00:41:33.090 --> 00:41:36.060
and devastation that I went through.

00:41:37.161 --> 00:41:42.166
- Yes, it was a shock, it was
definitely one of the issues

00:41:43.934 --> 00:41:45.636
I had to deal with in my life

00:41:45.636 --> 00:41:48.873
since I've been home for
a year and a half or so.

00:41:48.873 --> 00:41:53.844
But with the help of family
and friends, I'm managing,

00:41:56.080 --> 00:42:00.351
I'm managing and trying to get forward,

00:42:01.552 --> 00:42:02.920
move forward with my life.

00:42:05.322 --> 00:42:07.191
- [Narrator] Earl Mann
is left to contemplate

00:42:07.191 --> 00:42:10.728
a capital murder trial and
possible death sentence.

00:42:10.728 --> 00:42:14.465
He decides it's in his best
interest to plead guilty.

00:42:14.465 --> 00:42:17.935
- Hey, I need some hairspray,
does anybody got any?

00:42:17.935 --> 00:42:22.406
Today he is supposed to plead
guilty to aggravated murder,

00:42:22.406 --> 00:42:26.243
attempted aggravated murder,
and guilty to the rapes

00:42:26.243 --> 00:42:29.680
on both my mom and my niece.

00:42:29.680 --> 00:42:31.682
We gotta go.

00:42:31.682 --> 00:42:36.153
Oh, I'm just nerves,
nerves, nerves, nerves.

00:42:36.153 --> 00:42:37.488
- [April] Yeah, I'm startin' to get em.

00:42:37.488 --> 00:42:41.559
- I feel like I'm walking into a coliseum

00:42:41.559 --> 00:42:43.894
where I have to fight the lions.

00:42:47.665 --> 00:42:50.734
I first and foremost did not

00:42:50.734 --> 00:42:53.070
wanna put my niece through another trial.

00:42:53.070 --> 00:42:58.042
She's been very riddled by fear,

00:42:58.042 --> 00:43:00.277
and I think that just putting
her through that again,

00:43:00.277 --> 00:43:03.948
and the whole entire family,
would just be too much,

00:43:03.948 --> 00:43:05.583
too much for what he's worth.

00:43:05.583 --> 00:43:10.588
So we decided that the
guilty plea was satisfactory.

00:43:12.456 --> 00:43:17.461
He'll be in court, which
is terrifying to Brooke.

00:43:17.761 --> 00:43:22.633
She's been just a nervous wreck.

00:43:22.633 --> 00:43:24.435
And muster up that strength.

00:43:25.436 --> 00:43:29.139
I want, in the courtroom when we talk,

00:43:29.139 --> 00:43:31.976
I want people to remember
what happened to my mom.

00:43:31.976 --> 00:43:34.712
And this day, we can finally feel

00:43:34.712 --> 00:43:37.514
that she's at rest in peace.

00:43:44.054 --> 00:43:45.289
(gavel pounding)

00:43:45.289 --> 00:43:46.290
- [Woman] All rise.

00:43:47.224 --> 00:43:50.394
This court of common
peace is now in session.

00:43:50.394 --> 00:43:54.131
Your Honorable Judge Robert
M Gibbons is now presiding.

00:43:54.131 --> 00:43:55.065
- [Woman] Thank you, Your Honor.

00:43:55.065 --> 00:43:59.203
This is case number 2007062079,

00:43:59.203 --> 00:44:01.805
State of Ohio versus Earl Mann.

00:44:01.805 --> 00:44:04.375
- Judge, at this time Mr.
Mann is prepared to withdrawal

00:44:04.375 --> 00:44:07.511
his former plea of not
guilty, entering a guilty plea

00:44:07.511 --> 00:44:09.713
to the counts as outlined
by the State of Ohio.

00:44:09.713 --> 00:44:11.148
- Do you understand your rights

00:44:11.148 --> 00:44:12.983
and the effect of the guilty plea?

00:44:12.983 --> 00:44:16.086
How do you plead in the charge
of first aggravated murder?

00:44:16.086 --> 00:44:17.321
- Guilty.

00:44:17.321 --> 00:44:18.789
- Further understand your rights

00:44:18.789 --> 00:44:19.923
and the effect of the guilty plea,

00:44:19.923 --> 00:44:21.925
how do you plead to the
charge of attempted battery

00:44:21.925 --> 00:44:24.461
and murder as set forth in count three?

00:44:24.461 --> 00:44:26.130
- Guilty.

00:44:26.130 --> 00:44:27.698
- [Woman] Your Honor, there are a number

00:44:27.698 --> 00:44:30.668
of members of the victim's
family that would like to speak.

00:44:32.002 --> 00:44:36.106
- You murdered my mother.
(sobbing)

00:44:38.342 --> 00:44:43.313
You beat her, you strangled
her, you broke her neck.

00:44:43.614 --> 00:44:45.315
And then you raped her.

00:44:48.652 --> 00:44:52.756
And then you tried to do
it to a six year old child.

00:44:54.858 --> 00:44:59.863
You're a coward, you're a
murderer and you're a liar.

00:45:01.965 --> 00:45:04.468
I hope you are locked away
for the rest of your life

00:45:04.468 --> 00:45:06.336
like the animal that you are,

00:45:06.336 --> 00:45:09.740
and you live in a cage
for the rest of your life.

00:45:09.740 --> 00:45:13.243
And I hope you keep it
in the back of your mind

00:45:13.243 --> 00:45:15.713
that you will never, ever again

00:45:15.713 --> 00:45:18.716
walk a free man on this Earth.

00:45:18.716 --> 00:45:19.917
Because you know why?

00:45:19.917 --> 00:45:23.620
I gotcha, I found out what you were about

00:45:23.620 --> 00:45:25.322
and that's why we're here today.

00:45:28.192 --> 00:45:30.360
(sobbing)

00:45:33.764 --> 00:45:35.833
- I think you are such a coward.

00:45:35.833 --> 00:45:39.570
I was only six years old,
what could I have done to you?

00:45:39.570 --> 00:45:41.839
What can my grandma have done to you?

00:45:43.407 --> 00:45:46.143
And I'm sparing your life, I shouldn't be.

00:45:47.277 --> 00:45:49.146
You deserve the death penalty.

00:45:50.080 --> 00:45:53.584
I don't think you deserve one more breath.

00:46:19.610 --> 00:46:21.145
- I was glad to be able to see him

00:46:21.145 --> 00:46:23.881
and I knew I was gonna be
able to stand in front of him

00:46:25.015 --> 00:46:30.020
and talk to him, and I knew
he was gonna recall himself

00:46:30.721 --> 00:46:35.726
and flash back to Mansfield
Institution, the prison.

00:46:36.527 --> 00:46:39.563
And I knew it was gonna hurt him.

00:47:19.903 --> 00:47:24.041
- Mann, you and I have nothing
but courteous exchanges,

00:47:24.041 --> 00:47:27.277
and I don't mean that to change today.

00:47:27.277 --> 00:47:29.513
But it falls to me as the judge

00:47:30.747 --> 00:47:34.384
to express this community's
utter outrage at you.

00:47:34.384 --> 00:47:37.120
There seems to be a
depth of depravity in you

00:47:37.120 --> 00:47:39.523
that is beyond understanding.

00:47:40.557 --> 00:47:43.160
The poet Dante once spoke about hell

00:47:43.160 --> 00:47:45.996
as being frozen ice as its lowest depths.

00:47:47.197 --> 00:47:49.900
And Mr. Mann, I can only
say I find that in you.

00:47:49.900 --> 00:47:53.136
The exchanges I've had
with you and seeing you,

00:47:53.136 --> 00:47:55.272
I can only conclude, however reluctantly,

00:47:55.272 --> 00:47:58.242
because I hate to say this of anyone,

00:47:58.242 --> 00:48:00.644
that you are not fit to be in society

00:48:00.644 --> 00:48:03.313
with the rest of us and you will not be.

00:48:03.313 --> 00:48:06.483
(audience applauding)

00:48:08.051 --> 00:48:09.987
Please, please, please, please.

00:48:09.987 --> 00:48:13.991
I'd ask that however you
may feel, please don't react

00:48:13.991 --> 00:48:16.793
until this is all over and
you're out of the courtroom.

00:48:16.793 --> 00:48:19.029
- I don't need an apology from him.

00:48:20.397 --> 00:48:23.233
And if he did say he was
sorry, I wouldn't believe it.

00:48:24.067 --> 00:48:25.569
I wasn't lookin' for that,

00:48:25.569 --> 00:48:27.070
what I was lookin' for was for him

00:48:27.070 --> 00:48:30.507
to be in those shackles
for the rest of his life.

00:48:30.507 --> 00:48:32.075
That's what I got.

00:48:32.075 --> 00:48:33.677
- I'm hoping in time, yes,

00:48:33.677 --> 00:48:36.480
I'm hoping in time I will forgive him.

00:48:36.480 --> 00:48:38.282
- [Woman] How do you remain so calm?

00:48:39.116 --> 00:48:43.754
- Well, I learned that while incarcerated.

00:48:45.455 --> 00:48:49.559
It's not gonna help me
to be angry and irate.

00:48:50.694 --> 00:48:52.963
I learned that while incarcerated.

00:48:52.963 --> 00:48:53.797
- [Woman] How do you feel

00:48:53.797 --> 00:48:55.232
about him taking this plea agreement?

00:48:55.232 --> 00:48:58.268
- I believe he got it to
avoid the death penalty.

00:48:58.268 --> 00:49:01.104
There's no doubt in my
mind on that subject.

00:49:01.104 --> 00:49:06.109
But I believe he will
get what's coming to him.

00:49:07.377 --> 00:49:12.382
And hopefully, he will come
to terms with God Almighty,

00:49:13.750 --> 00:49:16.119
and before it's too late.

00:49:16.119 --> 00:49:18.455
- It was just great to
see the final chapter

00:49:18.455 --> 00:49:20.324
and the book finally come to a close,

00:49:20.324 --> 00:49:24.695
and to see the family get that closure.

00:49:24.695 --> 00:49:27.898
I guess one of the
messages I hope people take

00:49:27.898 --> 00:49:30.067
is exactly opposite what
the judge said today.

00:49:30.067 --> 00:49:31.668
The judge gave a speech about

00:49:31.668 --> 00:49:35.405
how that it always fixes
itself and it took awhile,

00:49:35.405 --> 00:49:38.842
but the wheels of justice
finally corrected themselves.

00:49:38.842 --> 00:49:41.144
And I think this case
shows the exact opposite,

00:49:41.144 --> 00:49:44.247
how many strokes of luck
have to happen in a row,

00:49:44.247 --> 00:49:46.616
and coincidences to happen in a row

00:49:46.616 --> 00:49:48.919
for someone to be able to do

00:49:48.919 --> 00:49:51.755
what Melinda and Clarence did here.

00:49:51.755 --> 00:49:53.156
And I think for the
vast majority of people

00:49:53.156 --> 00:49:54.791
who are sittin' in their position,

00:49:54.791 --> 00:49:58.695
all those cosmic
coincidences don't line up.

00:49:58.695 --> 00:50:01.198
(light music)

00:50:10.907 --> 00:50:15.112
- Well, Mom, this is it.

00:50:15.112 --> 00:50:18.482
We all love you and we want
you to rest in peace now.

00:50:21.218 --> 00:50:22.085
It's over.

00:50:28.425 --> 00:50:31.194
(Melinda crying)

00:50:43.840 --> 00:50:47.677
Oh, I do feel like there
is a lot of emotions.

00:50:49.379 --> 00:50:51.681
Generally I rollercoaster
when somethin' big

00:50:51.681 --> 00:50:54.117
like this happens, but this is phenomenal

00:50:54.117 --> 00:50:55.952
and I feel very happy.

00:50:59.122 --> 00:51:03.193
We finally have created the moment

00:51:08.265 --> 00:51:12.469
where we can all say it's over now,

00:51:12.469 --> 00:51:17.474
he's pleaded guilty and
we'll never forget my mother.

00:51:18.275 --> 00:51:22.546
Always, always keep her in
our thoughts and our memories.

00:51:24.948 --> 00:51:29.152
And I feel that she knows
and it's a big relief

00:51:30.921 --> 00:51:32.989
that she can rest in peace now.

00:51:35.192 --> 00:51:36.960
Dear Lord, thank you for this day.

00:51:36.960 --> 00:51:41.131
It's been a day mixed
with so many emotions

00:51:41.131 --> 00:51:44.701
and we thank you that
you brought us this far,

00:51:44.701 --> 00:51:47.938
with all the people,
friends and family involved,

00:51:47.938 --> 00:51:52.576
to bring down the man
who created this chaos.

00:51:53.477 --> 00:51:57.280
And Lord, give us the strength to go on

00:51:57.280 --> 00:52:02.285
and live our lives, and
let us help others, amen.

00:52:03.386 --> 00:52:04.488
- [All] Amen.

00:52:06.022 --> 00:52:08.458
(exhaling deeply)
(light music)

00:52:08.458 --> 00:52:11.795
(background chattering)

00:52:14.364 --> 00:52:17.901
- Oh yeah, I'm working
on reading a good book

00:52:18.802 --> 00:52:22.038
that caught my eye, and
what I've read thus far,

00:52:22.038 --> 00:52:25.308
it's pretty much my story.

00:52:25.308 --> 00:52:30.280
And it's sad, but it's
true, and it happens, so.

00:52:30.280 --> 00:52:32.883
I see a lot of myself in that book.

00:52:34.818 --> 00:52:39.823
I think at times that
people just don't understand

00:52:40.257 --> 00:52:45.262
what we as wrongfully incarcerated
individuals go through.

00:52:49.966 --> 00:52:52.402
I think it's a man thing, or a guy thing,

00:52:52.402 --> 00:52:57.107
that we think we can handle it on our own

00:52:57.107 --> 00:53:00.710
and get through it and tough it out,

00:53:00.710 --> 00:53:05.048
but I found out that that doesn't work.

00:53:05.048 --> 00:53:06.583
(metal clipping)

00:53:06.583 --> 00:53:11.254
Hopefully myself and the other victims

00:53:11.254 --> 00:53:14.491
may have some sort of comfort out of this,

00:53:14.491 --> 00:53:18.161
that the person that did commit this crime

00:53:18.161 --> 00:53:20.964
was brought to justice
and held accountable.

00:53:22.532 --> 00:53:27.537
So, yeah, the sun came out
and it's gonna be a good day.

